    text: "Every Saturday, Grandma Rosa baked a tray of oatmeal cookies and left it on the kitchen windowsill to cool. Nobody in the family would ever dare to interrupt this tradition. So when Nico got home from soccer practice and found the tray completely empty, he knew at once that a crime had been committed.\n\nNico considered himself the most observant detective on Maple Street, although, to be fair, he was also the only one. He opened his notebook and recorded three clues. There were crumbs scattered near the back door. There was a small, muddy print on the windowsill. And his little sister, Ana, had a suspicious brown smear on her chin.\n\n'Ana did it,' Nico announced confidently.\n\nAna rolled her eyes. 'That's chocolate from my candy bar. I would never eat oatmeal cookies. They taste like cardboard.'\n\nNico frowned, because he had to admit that she was right. Ana had refused oatmeal in every form since she was three years old.\n\nHe studied the print again. It was far too tiny to belong to Biscuit, their huge, lazy old dog. Besides, Biscuit was snoring under the table. Following the trail of crumbs, Nico crossed the yard until he reached the oak tree beside the fence.\n\nPerched on a branch sat a plump gray squirrel, clutching a cookie in its paws and nibbling frantically. Behind it, stuffed into a hollow in the trunk, Nico counted six more cookies.\n\n'Case closed,' Nico declared. Then he marched inside to make a recommendation: next Saturday, the cookies should cool somewhere that squirrels could not reach.",
