    id: 'r02', lv: 1, title: "The Toughest Animal on Earth",
    text: "Meet one of the toughest creatures on the planet. It is usually less than a millimeter long, so you would need a microscope to see it clearly. It has eight stubby legs with tiny claws on the ends. Scientists call it a tardigrade. But because it lumbers along like a chubby little bear, most people call it a water bear.\n\nTardigrades live almost everywhere. You can find them in damp moss, in sand dunes, on mountaintops, and on the deep ocean floor. If you scooped up some moss from your backyard and looked at it under a microscope, you might find a few of them.\n\nWhat makes water bears famous is their remarkable ability to survive. When their home dries out, a tardigrade pulls in its legs and curls into a shriveled ball called a tun. Then its body slows down until it has almost stopped. In this state, it can endure freezing cold, intense heat, and years without water. When water finally returns, the tiny animal swells up, stretches its legs, and walks away as if nothing had happened.\n\nIn 2007, researchers tested this toughness in an extreme way. They attached dried tardigrades to the outside of a spacecraft, where there was no air and no shield from harsh sunlight. Many of them survived the trip. Some later laid eggs that hatched into healthy babies.\n\nScientists now study how water bears protect their bodies. They hope the answers might someday help keep medicines safe without a fridge. Water bears are not superheroes, but they may be the closest thing nature has made.",
