    id: 'r03', lv: 1, title: "The Walk That Made Velcro",
    text: "In 1941, a Swiss engineer named George de Mestral took his dog for a hike in the mountains. When they got home, both of them were covered in burrs, the prickly seed pods of a plant called burdock. Most people would have simply picked them off and thrown them away. De Mestral, however, became curious about why they clung so stubbornly.\n\nHe put a burr under a microscope. He saw that it was covered in hundreds of tiny hooks. These hooks snagged on anything with a loop, such as the threads in his pants or his dog's fur. De Mestral wondered if people could make a fastener that worked the same way.\n\nTurning that idea into a real product was very hard. For about ten years, he experimented with different materials. Cotton was too soft and wore out quickly. Eventually, he discovered that nylon could form strong, durable hooks. One strip of fabric held the hooks, and the other held soft loops. Pressed together, they gripped tightly. Pulled apart, they made a familiar ripping sound.\n\nHe called his invention Velcro, combining two French words: velours, meaning velvet, and crochet, meaning hook. At first, many people considered it ugly and cheap-looking. Then NASA began using it to keep tools and food from floating away inside spacecraft. Suddenly it seemed exciting. Today you can find Velcro on shoes, jackets, and backpacks. It all started because one man refused to ignore an annoying walk in the woods.",
