    id: 'r06', lv: 2, title: "The Punch of the Mantis Shrimp",
    text: "If you were a crab living on a coral reef, the last creature you would want to meet is a mantis shrimp. Despite its name, it is neither a shrimp nor a praying mantis. It is a brightly colored crustacean, usually about as long as a human hand. It also has one of the fastest strikes in the animal kingdom.\n\nSome mantis shrimp, known as smashers, keep a pair of club-shaped limbs folded beneath their bodies. When they attack, these clubs shoot forward at roughly 50 miles per hour. The strike is so rapid that the water in front of the club cannot keep up, and tiny bubbles form. When those bubbles collapse, they release a second burst of force, along with heat and a faint flash of light. Consequently, a snail or crab is struck twice in a fraction of a second. In aquariums, large mantis shrimp have occasionally cracked the glass walls of their tanks.\n\nTheir eyes are equally unusual. Humans have three types of color-sensing cells, while mantis shrimp have between twelve and sixteen. Each eye sits on its own stalk. The animal can look up with one eye and sideways with the other. Surprisingly, experiments suggest that mantis shrimp are not especially good at telling similar colors apart. Scientists think their eyes may be built for speed rather than detail. This would let them recognize colors instantly instead of carefully comparing them.\n\nEngineers have also studied the smasher's club. It can survive thousands of violent blows without breaking. By copying its layered structure, they hope to build lighter, tougher materials for helmets and airplanes.",
