    id: 'r07', lv: 2, title: "Fog on Pine Lake",
    text: "Owen and his older cousin Tess had paddled their canoe to the far shore of Pine Lake, where the fish supposedly bit better. By the time they turned back, a dense white fog had drifted across the water, so thick that Owen could barely make out Tess's shape at the front of the canoe.\n\n'Which way is camp?' asked Tess, whose voice, usually so confident, now sounded small.\n\nOwen felt his heart hammering, because everything around them looked identical: gray water, gray air, gray nothing. Then he recalled something his grandfather had taught him the previous summer, while they were fishing on this same lake. Sound travels well across water, his grandfather had said, and when you are lost, you should stop and listen before you move.\n\n'Stop paddling,' Owen said, trying to sound calmer than he felt.\n\nThey sat motionless while water lapped gently against the hull. A loon called somewhere to their left. Then, faintly, Owen detected something else: a steady metallic clang, repeating again and again.\n\n'The dinner bell,' he whispered. Every evening at six, Mr. Alvarez rang the iron bell outside the camp kitchen, though usually only a few times.\n\nThey aimed the canoe toward the sound and paddled cautiously, pausing every few strokes to make sure they were still heading the right way. Gradually the clanging grew louder, until a dark shape emerged from the fog: the wooden dock, with Mr. Alvarez standing at the very end, still swinging the bell rope.\n\n'I kept ringing once the fog rolled in,' he said, rubbing his aching arm. 'Figured somebody might need it.'\n\nTess scrambled out and hugged him. Owen remained in the canoe a moment longer, listening to the quiet, and decided he would call his grandfather that night.",
