    text: "When Wilma Rudolph was born in Tennessee in 1940, she weighed only four and a half pounds. She was the twentieth of twenty-two children. She was also often sick. At age four, she contracted polio, a disease that left her left leg weak and twisted. Doctors predicted that she might never walk normally.\n\nHer family, however, refused to accept that prediction. Every week, Wilma and her mother rode a bus about fifty miles to a hospital in Nashville. It was one of the nearest places that would treat Black patients. At home, her brothers and sisters took turns massaging her leg several times a day. For years, Wilma wore a heavy metal brace. Around age twelve, she finally took it off for good.\n\nOnce she could run, she seemed determined to make up for lost time. She became a basketball star in high school, and a college track coach named Ed Temple noticed her remarkable speed. At just sixteen, she earned a bronze medal in a relay race at the 1956 Olympics.\n\nFour years later, at the 1960 Olympics in Rome, Rudolph became the first American woman to win three gold medals in track and field at a single Olympics. Reporters around the world called her the fastest woman alive.\n\nWhen she returned home, her town of Clarksville planned a parade in her honor. At the time, much of the South was segregated, meaning that Black and white people were kept apart by law. Rudolph announced that she would not attend unless the celebration was open to everyone. Consequently, the parade and the banquet that followed became the first integrated events in the town's history.",
