    id: 'r09', lv: 2, title: "The Missing Trophy",
    text: "On Monday morning, the glass case outside the principal's office was empty, because the gold trophy from the state spelling bee, the pride of Hillside Middle School, had vanished.\n\nBy lunchtime, rumors were spreading faster than the cafeteria could serve pizza. Some students blamed the team from Riverview, which had lost the final round. Others whispered about Mr. Pike, the custodian, simply because he carried keys to every door. Priya, who had won the bee, felt slightly sick, since technically it was her trophy.\n\nRather than join the gossip, Priya decided to investigate. She noticed immediately that the lock on the case was undamaged, which meant that whoever had opened it had used a key. She also discovered a small white card wedged behind the case that read 'Order 4471, ready Friday.'\n\nWhen Priya showed the card to her best friend, Dmitri, he frowned and asked what kind of order it could be.\n\nAfter school, the two of them walked along Main Street, peering into every shop window. At the third store, Gold Star Engraving, Priya stopped abruptly, because on the counter, gleaming under the lights, sat the missing trophy.\n\nThe man behind the counter smiled when he noticed them. 'Your principal dropped this off on Saturday so that I could add a new plate to the base,' he explained. 'I believe it's meant to be a surprise at Friday's assembly.'\n\nHe rotated the trophy so they could read the polished new plate: 'State Champion: Priya Raman.'\n\nPriya laughed so hard that she had to sit down on the shop's front step. Then she thought guiltily about Mr. Pike, whose name had been passed around all day without a shred of evidence. Before they left, she and Dmitri made two promises. They would act completely astonished on Friday, and tomorrow, without spoiling the surprise, they would quietly stop the rumors about Mr. Pike.",
