    id: 'r10', lv: 2, title: "The Dance of the Honeybee",
    text: "A honeybee that discovers a field full of flowers faces a challenge. How can she inform the thousands of other bees in her hive where the food is located? She cannot draw a map, and she cannot point. Instead, she dances.\n\nBack inside the dark hive, the forager climbs onto the honeycomb, which hangs straight up and down. There she performs what scientists call the waggle dance. She runs forward in a straight line while vibrating her body rapidly from side to side. Then she circles back to her starting point and repeats the run again and again. Her path traces a pattern shaped roughly like a figure eight.\n\nEvery part of this performance carries information. The angle of the straight run indicates direction, measured in relation to the sun. If she waggles straight up the comb, the flowers lie in the direction of the sun. If she waggles at an angle to the right of straight up, the flowers lie at that same angle to the right of the sun. The duration of the waggle communicates distance: the longer she waggles, the farther away the food is.\n\nThe bees crowding around her cannot see well in the darkness. Instead, they follow the dance by touching her with their antennae, and they smell the scent of the flowers on her body. Then they fly out to locate the food themselves.\n\nAn Austrian scientist named Karl von Frisch spent decades watching bees to decode this language. At first, some experts doubted his conclusions. Eventually his experiments convinced most of them, and in 1973 he shared the Nobel Prize. He had demonstrated that an insect with a brain smaller than a sesame seed could communicate precise directions through movement alone.",
