    id: 'r11', lv: 3, title: "Apollo 13: A Successful Failure",
    text: "On April 13, 1970, three astronauts were roughly 200,000 miles from Earth, hurtling toward the moon, when they heard a sharp bang. One of the oxygen tanks in the service module had exploded. Moments later, Jack Swigert radioed Mission Control in Houston with a calm understatement: 'Houston, we've had a problem here.'\n\nThe command module, Odyssey, needed that oxygen to make electricity. Now its power was rapidly draining away. The crew, Jim Lovell, Jack Swigert, and Fred Haise, abandoned any hope of a moon landing. Their new mission was simply to survive.\n\nTheir lifeboat became Aquarius, the small lunar module that was supposed to carry two of them to the surface of the moon. It had been designed to support two men for about two days. Now it had to sustain three men for nearly four. To conserve power, the crew shut down almost every system, including the heaters. The cabin grew almost as cold as a refrigerator, and water collected on the walls and windows.\n\nThen a less visible threat emerged. With every breath, the astronauts were breathing out carbon dioxide, a gas that can be deadly at high levels. Aquarius carried filters to absorb the gas, but not nearly enough for three people over four days. Odyssey had plenty of spare filters. However, they were square, while the openings in Aquarius were round. Working around the clock, engineers on the ground designed an adapter. They used only materials they knew were on board, such as plastic bags, cardboard covers, a sock, and duct tape. They then read the instructions to the crew, step by step, over the radio. The carbon dioxide levels began to fall.\n\nOn April 17, the astronauts climbed back into Odyssey, released Aquarius, and plunged through the atmosphere. Millions of people watched anxiously until three orange and white parachutes blossomed above the Pacific Ocean. Lovell later called the flight a 'successful failure,' a phrase that captured both what had been lost and what had been achieved.",
